From: skyjet18 <57456827+skyjet18@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 08:29:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 46/49] hls: improved handling of event type playlist

- allow seeking in event playlist: when a playlist is selected during
  playback (cur_timestamp set), an EVENT playlist is matched by
  timestamp like a finished playlist, so a timestamp seek works

Ported to FFmpeg 9.0.2 from 0029-hls-improved-handling-of-event-type-playlist.patch
(0026 of the FFmpeg 8.0 set). Seeking in EVENT playlists and the
duration of EVENT playlists (in hls_read_header() and, while the
playlist grows, at the end of parse_playlist()) are already in 9.0.2,
so update_duration() is not added. Only the timestamp match in
select_cur_seq_no() is ported.
Review: the "start from the beginning" part of the original is dropped.
Without a seek an EVENT playlist starts like upstream (live_start_index
honoured, default three segments before the live edge).
---
 libavformat/hls.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libavformat/hls.c b/libavformat/hls.c
index c5c68e44..6f1aacb2 100644
--- a/libavformat/hls.c
+++ b/libavformat/hls.c
@@ -2114,7 +2114,7 @@ static int64_t select_cur_seq_no(HLSContext *c, struct playlist *pls)
     /* If playback is already in progress (we are just selecting a new
      * playlist) and this is a complete file, find the matching segment
      * by counting durations. */
-    if (pls->finished && c->cur_timestamp != AV_NOPTS_VALUE) {
+    if ((pls->finished || pls->type == PLS_TYPE_EVENT) && c->cur_timestamp != AV_NOPTS_VALUE) {
         find_timestamp_in_playlist(c, pls, c->cur_timestamp, &seq_no, NULL);
         return seq_no;
     }
